var e={"singular-framework-core":{slug:`singular-framework-core`,label:`Case study · 2025 · Open source · MIT`,headline:`A framework family for single-object storage.`,lede:`Entity Framework handles tables of objects. Singular Framework Core handles the other case: one object, stored on its own, with the same CRUD interface you would expect from a database but for a config file, a local state file, or anything else that lives by itself.`,quickFacts:[{label:`Role`,value:`Author · Solo`},{label:`Duration`,value:`2025 — ongoing`},{label:`Packages`,value:`5+ on NuGet`},{label:`Stack`,value:`C# · .NET Standard 2.0+`},{label:`License`,value:`MIT`}],problem:{statement:`Every project I built needed to store a single object. There was never a clean way to do it.`,explanation:`Load it. Validate it. Transform it. Encrypt it if it needs it. Serialize it. Write it somewhere. Version it when the shape changes. Migrate it when the version changes. Every one of those steps is the same across every project, and none of them are what the project is actually about. I kept rewriting the same six-step pipeline, badly, and I finally built the library I wished existed.`},approach:{title:`What I built.`,bullets:[`Core library defines the interfaces. Every backend, serializer, and processor is a separate NuGet package.`,`CRUD-based API: Create, Read, Update, Delete — with async and bulk variants.`,`Four-phase storage pipeline: pre-processing → serialization → post-processing → storage.`,`Processing layers composable: encrypt, reshape, modify properties, or add custom transforms.`,`Generic over input and output types.`],flow:[`Program`,`Pre-Process`,`Serialize`,`Post-Process`,`Storage`]},results:{title:`What shipped.`,stats:[{value:`5+`,label:`NuGet packages published`},{value:`1.0.0`,label:`SharpCrud stable release`},{value:`MIT`,label:`License · open source`}],note:`Each package is versioned independently, following the same interface-versioning discipline as the core.`},decisions:[{id:`ADR-001`,category:`Architecture`,title:`Interface-first package family over a single monolithic library`,problem:`Support multiple storage backends, serialization formats, and encryption algorithms without forcing every consumer to pull in everything.`,options:`One library with everything · core + extension packages · DI container in one assembly.`,choice:`Core defines interfaces. Every backend is a separate NuGet package. Consumers install only what they use.`,consequence:`Package count went from 1 to 5+. Bundle size for typical consumers dropped to what they actually call. Third-party extension became possible without forking.`,rejected:`A single monolithic library would have been simpler to publish, but it would have forced every consumer to pull in code they never call.`},{id:`ADR-002`,category:`Processing pipeline`,title:`Four-phase composable pipeline over a fixed serialization chain`,problem:`Different projects need different transforms: encrypt the payload, reshape the bytes, modify properties before storage. A fixed chain could not cover all of them.`,options:`Hard-code serialization · expose a serializer interface only · four-phase pipeline with pluggable processors at each stage.`,choice:`Four phases — pre-processing, serialization, post-processing, storage — each with its own interface. Any processor can be inserted at any stage.`,consequence:`Encryption became a processor. Binary reshaping became a processor. Property modification became a processor. Adding a new transform is a DI change, not a library fork.`},{id:`ADR-003`,category:`Generic typing`,title:`Generic over input and output`,problem:`The stored format is not always the in-memory format. A model might serialize to JSON, BSON, or a custom binary format depending on the backend.`,options:`Fixed output type · object as output · generic output type.`,choice:`Generic. The input is the model. The output is the stored format. Both are defined by the consumer.`,consequence:`The same core works for JSON, BSON, binary, or anything else. Serializers are interchangeable. The type system enforces the contract at compile time.`}],lessons:[{title:`Publish the core first, add extensions later`,body:`I designed all the packages in parallel. In hindsight, publishing the core alone and letting the interfaces stabilize under real use would have made the extension API cleaner.`},{title:`Write the interface documentation before the implementations`,body:`The core interfaces changed more than they should have once I started writing the concrete backends. Writing the docs first would have surfaced the awkward seams earlier.`},{title:`Underestimated version coordination across packages`,body:`Five packages means five version numbers, five changelogs, and a compatibility matrix. It is manageable, but I would build tooling for it earlier next time.`}],comparison:{title:`When to use Singular vs alternatives`,description:`Singular is not the right tool for every problem. Here is where it fits and where it does not.`,columnLabel:`Singular Framework Core`,alternativesLabel:`Alternatives`,rows:[{label:`Use case`,thisProject:`One object stored independently — config files, local state, user preferences`,alternatives:`Entity Framework for tables; manual serialization for ad-hoc files`},{label:`Storage backends`,thisProject:`Pluggable: file system, cloud, in-memory, custom`,alternatives:`Fixed per library`},{label:`Encryption`,thisProject:`Built-in composable layer, any BCL symmetric algorithm`,alternatives:`Manual, or a separate library`},{label:`Versioning`,thisProject:`Graph-based migrations with automatic path resolution`,alternatives:`Manual version checks, or no versioning`},{label:`Right fit`,thisProject:`Projects that repeatedly store a single typed object`,alternatives:`Projects with relational data, or one-off file IO`}]},faqs:[{question:`What is Singular Framework Core?`,answer:`Singular Framework Core is a .NET library for storing and managing a single object independently — such as a config file, a local state file, or a user preferences object. It provides a CRUD-based interface over a composable pipeline of serialization, encryption, and storage layers, so the same code works whether the object ends up in a file, in the cloud, or in memory.`},{question:`How is it different from Entity Framework Core?`,answer:`Entity Framework Core manages tables of objects — relational data with rows, columns, and joins. Singular Framework Core manages a single object stored on its own, with no relations. They solve different problems and can be used side by side in the same project.`},{question:`Can I use it with any storage backend?`,answer:`Yes. The core library defines only the interfaces. Storage is provided by extension packages — file system, and soon cloud and in-memory. Any third party can add a new backend by implementing the storage interface.`},{question:`Does it support encryption?`,answer:`Yes. Encryption is a composable processor in the pipeline. Any symmetric algorithm in the .NET base class library works through the same interface, and the pipeline can chain multiple processors — encrypt, then reshape, then store.`},{question:`What license is it under?`,answer:`MIT. Free for commercial and personal use.`}],repository:`https://github.com/MohammadAyaad/SingularFrameworkCore`,license:`https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT`,language:`C#`,code:{title:`What using it looks like.`,language:`csharp`,code:`public class AppConfig
{
    public string ApiKey { get; set; }
    public int TimeoutSeconds { get; set; }
}

var singular = new Singular<AppConfig, string>(
    serializer: new NewtonsoftJsonSerializer<AppConfig>(),
    storage: new FileSystemTextRepository("config.json"),
    processors: new IDataProcessorLayer<AppConfig, string>[]
    {
        new SymmetricEncryptorLayer<AppConfig, string>()
    }
);

await singular.CreateAsync(new AppConfig { ApiKey = "...", TimeoutSeconds = 30 });
var config = await singular.ReadAsync();
await singular.UpdateAsync(new AppConfig { ApiKey = "...", TimeoutSeconds = 60 });
await singular.DeleteAsync();`},related:[`entity-migration-framework`,`cleaner-sharp-api`]},"entity-migration-framework":{slug:`entity-migration-framework`,label:`Case study · 2025 · Open source · MIT`,headline:`Version migration by graph, not by list.`,lede:`Entities evolve. A UserV1 becomes a UserV2, which becomes a UserV3. Most migration systems make you register every pair of versions you might jump between. This one makes you register each migration once, as an edge in a graph, and finds the path from wherever you are to wherever you need to be.`,quickFacts:[{label:`Role`,value:`Author · Solo`},{label:`Duration`,value:`2025 — ongoing`},{label:`Packages`,value:`1 on NuGet`},{label:`Stack`,value:`C# · .NET Standard 2.0+`},{label:`License`,value:`MIT`}],problem:{statement:`Migrating from version 3 to version 7 should not require registering every combination between them.`,explanation:`A flat migration list forces you to write pairwise transformations: v3 to v4, v4 to v5, v5 to v6, v6 to v7, and then every other combination someone might need. It works until it does not. Miss a pair, and the migration silently fails at runtime. The graph-based approach fixes this by registering each migration once as a directed edge, then finding the shortest path from source to target. If a path does not exist, it fails loudly at registration time, not silently in production.`},approach:{title:`What I built.`,bullets:[`Each migration is an edge: a generic type pairing a source version with a target version.`,`The registry is a directed graph. Path resolution is automatic.`,`Compile-time validated transformations — the migrate method returns the target type, not object.`,`Missing paths fail at registration, not at runtime.`,`Extracted from Singular Framework Core, usable standalone in any .NET project.`],flow:[`UserV1`,`UserV2`,`UserV3`,`UserV4`]},results:{title:`What shipped.`,stats:[{value:`1`,label:`NuGet package`},{value:`Early alpha`,label:`Active development`},{value:`MIT`,label:`License · open source`}],note:`Register v1 to v2, v2 to v3, v3 to v4. The framework composes them automatically when you ask for v1 to v4.`},decisions:[{id:`ADR-001`,category:`Migration registry`,title:`Graph-based registry with path finding over a flat list`,problem:`Entities evolve through many versions. Migrating from v3 to v7 requires composing v3 to v4, v4 to v5, v5 to v6, v6 to v7, and not every direct jump has a registered migration.`,options:`Flat list applied sequentially · pairwise registrations · graph with path resolution.`,choice:`Graph. Each migration registers as a directed edge between two versions. The framework finds the path from source to target.`,consequence:`New versions register one edge, not every pairwise combination. Missing paths fail loudly at registration instead of silently at runtime.`,rejected:`A flat list grows quadratically with each new version, and pairwise registration turns schema evolution into busywork.`},{id:`ADR-002`,category:`Type safety`,title:`Compile-time validated transformations over runtime casting`,problem:`A migration takes one type and returns another. Without generics, the return type is object and every consumer has to cast.`,options:`Object-based migration · reflection-based mapping · generic migration pairing source and target types.`,choice:`Generic. The compiler knows both the source and target type, and the migrate method returns the target type directly.`,consequence:`No casts. No runtime type errors from migration mismatches. The compiler catches problems before the code runs.`},{id:`ADR-003`,category:`Packaging`,title:`Standalone package over an internal feature of Singular`,problem:`The migration layer was built inside Singular Framework Core, but the problem it solves is not specific to single-object persistence.`,options:`Keep it as an internal module · extract as a standalone library · duplicate into both.`,choice:`Extract as a standalone NuGet package that Singular depends on.`,consequence:`Any .NET project can use migration independently. Singular gets the same code without an internal fork, and the API surface stays minimal in both.`}],comparison:{title:`When to use graph-based migrations`,description:`Graph-based migration is not always the answer. Here is where it earns its complexity.`,columnLabel:`Entity Migration Framework`,alternativesLabel:`Flat-list migrations`,rows:[{label:`Complexity`,thisProject:`Each migration registers once, as an edge`,alternatives:`Each pair of versions needs its own entry`},{label:`Failure mode`,thisProject:`Missing paths fail at registration — you know before you ship`,alternatives:`Missing registrations fail at runtime — you find out in production`},{label:`Version count`,thisProject:`Scales to dozens of versions without pairwise explosion`,alternatives:`Pairwise count grows quadratically`},{label:`Type safety`,thisProject:`Compile-time validated transformations`,alternatives:`Runtime casts and manual checks`},{label:`Right fit`,thisProject:`Schemas that evolve often, or that have many reachable versions`,alternatives:`One-time migrations, or schemas with fewer than three versions`}]},lessons:[{title:`Document the path resolution algorithm before implementing it`,body:`I spent more time debugging edge cases in the graph traversal than I should have. Writing the algorithm down on paper first would have surfaced the tricky cases earlier.`},{title:`Provide a default registry out of the box`,body:`The library requires you to build your own registry. That is flexible, but it is also friction. A sensible default would make the first run smoother.`},{title:`Ship the documentation site earlier`,body:`The documentation is still incomplete. For a library, the docs are half the product. I would treat them as a first-class deliverable from day one.`}],faqs:[{question:`What is Entity Migration Framework?`,answer:`Entity Migration Framework is a .NET library for migrating an entity through its version history. Instead of registering every pair of versions you might jump between, each migration is registered once as a directed edge in a graph. The framework finds the shortest path from the source version to the target version automatically.`},{question:`Why use a graph instead of a flat list?`,answer:`A flat list requires you to register every pairwise combination of versions, which grows quadratically as the schema evolves. A graph registers each migration once, and the framework composes them. It also fails loudly at registration time if a path does not exist, rather than failing silently at runtime.`},{question:`Is it type safe?`,answer:`Yes. Each migration is generic over its input and output types. The migration method returns the target type directly, so there are no runtime casts and no type errors from migration mismatches.`},{question:`Can it be used outside of Singular?`,answer:`Yes. It was extracted from the migration layer of Singular Framework Core, but it is a standalone library. Any .NET project that needs version-aware entity migration can use it independently.`},{question:`What is the license?`,answer:`MIT.`}],code:{title:`What using it looks like.`,language:`csharp`,code:`public class UserV1ToV2 : IMigration<UserV1, UserV2>
{
    public UserV2 Migrate(UserV1 src) => new()
    {
        FullName = $"{src.FirstName} {src.LastName}"
    };
}

var manager = new MigrationBuilder<User>()
    .AddMigration<UserV1ToV2, UserV1, UserV2>()
    .Build();

var v2User = manager.Migrate<UserV1, UserV2>(v1User);
var v3User = manager.Migrate<UserV1, UserV3>(v1User); // composed automatically`},related:[`singular-framework-core`,`cleaner-sharp-api`],repository:`https://github.com/MohammadAyaad/EntityMigrationFramework`,license:`https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT`,language:`C#`},"cleaner-sharp-api":{slug:`cleaner-sharp-api`,label:`Case study · 2025 · Open source · MIT`,headline:`A base for writing cleaner C# APIs.`,lede:`Every API has the same three layers: data access, business logic, and request handling. Most projects rebuild them from scratch. CleanerSharpApi gives you the interfaces so you can focus on the code that is actually unique to your product.`,quickFacts:[{label:`Role`,value:`Author · Solo`},{label:`Duration`,value:`2025 — ongoing`},{label:`Packages`,value:`1 on NuGet`},{label:`Stack`,value:`C# · .NET Standard 2.0+`},{label:`License`,value:`MIT`}],problem:{statement:`Clean architecture is a good idea that is tedious to implement from scratch.`,explanation:`You know the layers you want: repositories that talk to storage, services that hold business logic, endpoints that handle HTTP. But every project you start, you write the same base classes again. The interfaces drift. The separation blurs. Six months in, the service layer is doing data access and the repository is doing business logic, and nobody remembers which one was supposed to own what. CleanerSharpApi is the base I kept rewriting.`},approach:{title:`What I built.`,bullets:[`Three-layer architecture: Repository, Service, Endpoint.`,`CRUD-first interface design. Sync, async, bulk, and bulk-async variants.`,`Every layer inherits from the same base, differentiated by responsibility.`,`Designed to slot into the ASP.NET pipeline after auth and general middleware.`,`Compile-time contracts for every layer boundary.`],flow:[`API Pipeline`,`Endpoint`,`Service`,`Repository`,`Storage`]},results:{title:`What shipped.`,stats:[{value:`1`,label:`NuGet package`},{value:`3`,label:`Architectural layers`},{value:`MIT`,label:`License · open source`}],note:`Small API surface, high reuse. New endpoints follow a known pattern from day one.`},decisions:[{id:`ADR-001`,category:`Layer separation`,title:`Same base interface for every layer, differentiated by responsibility`,problem:`Repository, Service, and Endpoint all do CRUD. If they share one interface, what stops them from being interchangeable?`,options:`Distinct interfaces per layer · shared base with no differentiation · shared base differentiated by name and usage.`,choice:`Shared base interface. Every layer inherits it. The differentiation is the layer responsibility, not the interface signature.`,consequence:`The API surface is smaller and more predictable. Developers know what every layer can do. The discipline comes from architecture, not from type signatures.`},{id:`ADR-002`,category:`CRUD variants`,title:`Four CRUD interface variants: sync, async, bulk sync, bulk async`,problem:`Different call sites need different shapes. A background job wants async. A batch process wants bulk. A quick check wants sync.`,options:`Only async · only sync · separate interfaces per shape.`,choice:`Four interfaces: sync, async, bulk sync, bulk async. Each inherits from the base actions it needs.`,consequence:`Consumers pick the shape that fits. Nothing is forced. The interface hierarchy stays flat and readable.`},{id:`ADR-003`,category:`Dependency injection`,title:`Unopinionated over a specific container`,problem:`Forcing a specific DI container would alienate teams that already use Autofac, or manual resolution, or Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.`,options:`Ship a specific container integration · require manual registration · leave DI entirely to the consumer.`,choice:`Leave DI to the consumer. The library defines interfaces only — no container, no registration helpers.`,consequence:`Adoption is trivial in any .NET project. The library stays small and does not pull in a container as a dependency.`}],comparison:{title:`When to use CleanerSharpApi`,description:`A three-layer API architecture is not the only way. Here is where it fits.`,columnLabel:`CleanerSharpApi`,alternativesLabel:`Manual layering`,rows:[{label:`Layer boundaries`,thisProject:`Enforced by interfaces: Repository to Service to Endpoint`,alternatives:`Enforced by convention and code review`},{label:`CRUD shapes`,thisProject:`Four variants provided: sync, async, bulk sync, bulk async`,alternatives:`Hand-written per entity`},{label:`Onboarding`,thisProject:`New endpoints follow a known pattern`,alternatives:`Every developer invents their own structure`},{label:`Right fit`,thisProject:`RESTful APIs with clear domain boundaries and multiple entities`,alternatives:`Small single-endpoint services, or GraphQL and gRPC-first APIs`}]},lessons:[{title:`Ship with a sample project`,body:`The library defines the shape, but a working example would show the shape in use. I would build a reference API alongside the library next time.`},{title:`Decide on the DI story earlier`,body:`How consumers register their implementations matters. I left that implicit. It should be explicit and documented.`},{title:`Better package naming`,body:`CleanerSharpApi is descriptive but long. Something shorter would be easier to type and remember.`}],faqs:[{question:`What is CleanerSharpApi?`,answer:`CleanerSharpApi is a .NET library that provides base interfaces for building RESTful APIs with clean separation between Repository, Service, and Endpoint layers. It exists to eliminate the boilerplate of writing the same three-layer structure for every entity in every project.`},{question:`Why three layers and not two?`,answer:`Two-layer APIs — endpoint calling repository directly — tend to accumulate business logic in the endpoint over time. The three-layer split keeps data access, business logic, and request handling separate, so each can evolve independently.`},{question:`Does it force a specific dependency injection setup?`,answer:`No. The interfaces are unopinionated. You register implementations the way your project already does — Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection, Autofac, or manual. The library does not require a particular container.`},{question:`Can I use only part of it?`,answer:`Yes. The interfaces are independent. You can use the Repository layer alone, or the Service layer, or the full stack. There is no all-or-nothing requirement.`},{question:`What is the license?`,answer:`MIT.`}],code:{title:`What using it looks like.`,language:`csharp`,code:`// Repository \u2014 data access
public interface IUserRepository : ICrudAsync<User, UserInput, UserUpdate>
{
}

// Service \u2014 business logic
public class UserService : IUserService
{
    private readonly IUserRepository _repo;
    public UserService(IUserRepository repo) => _repo = repo;

    public Task<User> CreateAsync(UserInput input) =>
        _repo.CreateAsync(input with { CreatedAt = DateTime.UtcNow });
}

// Endpoint \u2014 request handling
public class CreateUserEndpoint : IEndpoint
{
    public void Map(IEndpointRouteBuilder app) =>
        app.MapPost("/users", async (UserInput input, IUserService service) =>
            Results.Ok(await service.CreateAsync(input)));
}`},related:[`singular-framework-core`,`entity-migration-framework`],repository:`https://github.com/MohammadAyaad/CleanerSharpApi`,license:`https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT`,language:`C#`},vtt:{slug:`vtt`,label:`Case study · 2024–2026 · Open source`,headline:`Tasks tracked by version, not by project.`,lede:`Most task trackers organize by project or by sprint. VTT organizes by release — every task belongs to a version, every version tracks its tasks, and cutting a release is a query instead of a reconstruction.`,quickFacts:[{label:`Role`,value:`Author · Solo`},{label:`Duration`,value:`2024 — ongoing`},{label:`Projects`,value:`2 · core + CLI`},{label:`Stack`,value:`C# · .NET`},{label:`Status`,value:`In development · alpha`}],problem:{statement:`Task tools organize around projects. Releases do not.`,explanation:`Every task tool I used organized around projects, sprints, or boards — not the version a task ships in. When a release went out, assembling what was in it meant querying multiple tools and reconciling by hand. VTT starts from the release: every task belongs to a version, every version knows its tasks, and shipping is a read instead of a reconstruction.`},approach:{title:`What I built.`,bullets:["Two projects: VersionTaskTracker (core) and VersionTaskTracker.Cli (the `vtt` executable).",`The core defines the domain model: tasks, versions, workspaces, release composition.`,`The CLI is the terminal interface: create, assign, close, and query tasks by version.`,`A separate private Rust reimplementation is in progress, exploring a workspace-first model across repos.`,`The core is usable from any .NET context. There is no GUI.`],flow:[`Task`,`Version`,`Workspace`,`Release`]},results:{title:`What shipped.`,stats:[{value:`2`,label:`Projects · core + CLI`},{value:`0.3.4-alpha`,label:`Latest release`},{value:`In development`,label:`Active`}],note:`Early releases are still alpha — the domain model is being refined before a stable tag.`},decisions:[{id:`ADR-001`,category:`Interface`,title:`CLI-first over GUI-first over library-only`,problem:`The task tracker could ship as a GUI app, a library, or a CLI. Each choice sets a different ceiling on what the tool can become.`,options:`GUI-first (desktop or web) · library-only · CLI-first with a library core.`,choice:`CLI-first. The core library (VersionTaskTracker) is independent and usable from any .NET context. The CLI (VersionTaskTracker.Cli) is the primary interface. No GUI exists.`,consequence:`The tool works where developers already are — the terminal. No UI to maintain, no design system to build, no platform-specific rendering. The library core means the same logic can be wrapped in a GUI later without a rewrite.`,rejected:`A GUI-first build would have front-loaded weeks of interface work before the domain model was stable. The domain model is the hard part; the interface is the easy part. CLI-first lets the hard part evolve without dragging a UI along.`}],lessons:[{title:`Versioning feels like metadata until it is the model`,body:`The first draft treated version as a property of the task. It was a real refactor to make version the primary key instead. Once it was, most of the CLI verbs collapsed into simpler queries.`},{title:`CLI ergonomics age faster than the domain`,body:`The command names that felt obvious on day one felt awkward by the third iteration. I would write the CLI verbs down as a spec before writing any handlers.`},{title:`Two projects still means coordinated releases`,body:`Splitting the core and the CLI means version coordination is real work. It was manageable at this scale, but any larger and the tooling matters more than the code.`}],faqs:[{question:`What is VTT?`,answer:`VTT (Version Task Tracker) is a .NET CLI and library for tracking tasks per software release. Instead of organizing tasks by project or sprint, every task belongs to a version, and cutting a release becomes a query rather than a manual reconciliation.`},{question:`Why not just use GitHub Issues or Jira?`,answer:`Both organize around projects, not releases. Getting "what is in version 0.4.0" out of either requires querying by label, milestone, or filter — and reconciling across tools if you use more than one. VTT makes the version the primary key.`},{question:`Is it a GUI or a CLI?`,answer:`CLI-first. VersionTaskTracker.Cli is the terminal interface. The core library (VersionTaskTracker) is usable in any .NET context if you want to integrate it elsewhere. No GUI exists.`},{question:`Is there a license?`,answer:`No license file is currently set in the repository.`}],code:{title:`CLI usage.`,language:`bash`,code:`# Add a task to a version
vtt task add "Fix auth bug" --version 0.4.0

# See what is in a version
vtt version show 0.4.0 --tasks

# Cut a release
vtt version release 0.4.0`},related:[`sharp-money-manager`,`contacts-manager`],repository:`https://github.com/MohammadAyaad/VersionTaskTracker`,language:`C#`},"sharp-money-manager":{slug:`sharp-money-manager`,label:`Case study · 2025 · Personal · In development`,headline:`Five platforms, one codebase: an experiment.`,lede:`A personal finance app that runs as a native install on Windows and Mac, on iOS and Android, and in the browser, all from one C# codebase. This was an experiment in cross-platform reach. A rebuild is planned with a different architecture.`,quickFacts:[{label:`Role`,value:`Author · Solo`},{label:`Duration`,value:`2025 — ongoing`},{label:`Platforms`,value:`5 · Win · Mac · iOS · Android · Web`},{label:`Stack`,value:`.NET MAUI Hybrid · Blazor · MudBlazor`},{label:`Status`,value:`Experiment · rebuild planned`}],problem:{statement:`Finance apps split your life across devices that do not talk to each other.`,explanation:`Bank apps do not see investment apps. Desktop tools do not sync with mobile. Spreadsheets do not run on a phone. Personal finance is one problem that gets fragmented into five, one per platform. Sharp Money Manager started as an experiment in the opposite direction: one domain model, one set of components, five targets. The platform is a delivery detail, not a design constraint.`},approach:{title:`What I built.`,bullets:[`One C# codebase across Windows, Mac, iOS, Android, and Web.`,`.NET MAUI Hybrid with Blazor — the UI is web components rendered natively on desktop and mobile.`,`MudBlazor for Material Design, so the same components work on every target.`,`One domain model shared across the entire app, not five variants.`,`Material Design throughout — no platform-specific UI forks.`]},results:{title:`Current state.`,stats:[{value:`5`,label:`Platforms reached`},{value:`1`,label:`Codebase`},{value:`Experiment`,label:`Outcome`}],note:`The MAUI Hybrid path worked end-to-end on all five targets. The experiment proved the cross-platform reach. A rebuild is planned with a different architecture, the same multi-platform goal.`},decisions:[{id:`ADR-001`,category:`Architecture`,title:`MAUI Hybrid over native or Flutter for five-platform reach`,problem:`Five targets — Windows, Mac, iOS, Android, Web — needed the same app. Native meant five codebases. Flutter meant a Dart rewrite. Neither fit a solo project.`,options:`Native per platform · Flutter · React Native · .NET MAUI Hybrid with Blazor.`,choice:`.NET MAUI Hybrid with Blazor and MudBlazor. The UI is web components rendered in a native shell. One C# codebase, one domain model, five targets.`,consequence:`The experiment reached all five platforms. One codebase meant one place to fix a bug, one place to add a feature. The trade-off is that MAUI Hybrid is not transparent — each platform still needs its own debugging.`,rejected:`Native would have meant five apps and five maintenance paths for a solo engineer. Flutter and React Native both required rewriting the domain model in a different language. Staying in .NET kept the language, the tooling, and the domain model consistent.`}],lessons:[{title:`MAUI Hybrid is not free cross-platform`,body:`The promise is "write once, run everywhere." The reality is "write once, debug five times." Each platform has its own opinions about WebView rendering, file systems, and lifecycle. The abstraction is good, but it is not transparent.`},{title:`Material Design is a foundation, not a finish`,body:`MudBlazor gives you the primitives, but every screen still needs real design decisions. Using a design system saves time on the primitives and gives you no help at all with the layout, the flow, or the empty states.`},{title:`An experiment is allowed to end`,body:`This project proved what it needed to prove: one codebase can reach five platforms. The rebuild is not a failure of the experiment. It is what the experiment taught me to build next.`}],faqs:[{question:`What is Sharp Money Manager?`,answer:`A personal finance app that runs on Windows, Mac, iOS, Android, and the web from a single .NET MAUI Hybrid codebase. It was an experiment in cross-platform reach. A rebuild with a different architecture is planned.`},{question:`Why MAUI Hybrid instead of Flutter or React Native?`,answer:`Because the app is written in C#, and MAUI Hybrid lets the same Blazor components render natively across five targets. Flutter would have meant a Dart rewrite, React Native a JavaScript one. Staying in .NET kept the domain model, the tooling, and the language consistent.`},{question:`Is it available yet?`,answer:`No. It was an experiment. A rebuild with a different architecture is planned.`}],related:[`vtt`,`contacts-manager`],language:`C#`},"contacts-manager":{slug:`contacts-manager`,label:`Case study · 2024 · Personal · Team project · Shipped`,headline:`Search that forgives typos.`,lede:`Most contact managers require exact matches. Type "Moamed" for "Mohamed" and you get nothing. ContactsManager uses Levenshtein edit distance so a partial or typo'd query returns the closest matches, sorted by similarity.`,quickFacts:[{label:`Role`,value:`Backend / Database`},{label:`Duration`,value:`2024`},{label:`Team`,value:`6 engineers · ProGeeks`},{label:`Stack`,value:`Java · Swing · SQLite`},{label:`Status`,value:`Shipped`}],problem:{statement:`Contacts search should handle the way people actually type.`,explanation:`Every contact app I had used required the query to match a field exactly. Real queries are partial, misspelled, or phonetically approximated. Users reach for a name before they have typed it perfectly, so a search that requires exactness is a search that fails half the time. ContactsManager treats search as approximate matching, not exact filtering.`},approach:{title:`What I built.`,bullets:[`Java desktop application — Swing for the UI, SQLite for persistence.`,`Search by name, phone, or email. Closest matches sorted first.`,`Levenshtein edit distance as the similarity metric.`,`Full CRUD on contacts — create, read, update, delete.`,`Six-person team. I owned the backend and the database layer. The UI and search algorithm were split across the team.`]},results:{title:`What shipped.`,stats:[{value:`1`,label:`Desktop app delivered`},{value:`3`,label:`Search fields · name · phone · email`},{value:`6`,label:`Engineers · team project`}],note:`Delivered as a working desktop application. The ProGeeks team repository lists all six contributors.`},decisions:[{id:`ADR-001`,category:`Search`,title:`Levenshtein edit distance over prefix matching`,problem:`Contacts search needs to handle typos and partial matches. Prefix matching fails when the first letter is wrong, which is common in real queries.`,options:`Exact match · prefix match · substring match · Levenshtein edit distance.`,choice:`Levenshtein. Every query is compared against every contact, and the closest matches are returned in order.`,consequence:`Misspelled queries still surface the right contact. A query for "Moamed" finds "Mohamed" ranked ahead of unrelated entries.`,rejected:`Prefix matching was simpler and faster, but it failed on transposed letters, missing vowels, and any query that did not start with the right letter. On a contact list, that is most queries.`}],lessons:[{title:`Small data sets hide algorithmic cost`,body:`Levenshtein is O(nm) per comparison, which sounds expensive until you realize the contact list is small enough that it does not matter. Sometimes the "slower" algorithm is the right one because the actual bottleneck is elsewhere.`},{title:`Splitting responsibilities needs a written contract`,body:`The team split the work into UI and data layers. Without a written interface between them, integration took longer than it should have. The lesson carried into every project after this one.`}],faqs:[{question:`What is ContactsManager?`,answer:`A Java desktop contact manager with fuzzy search. Search by name, phone, or email, and the closest matches are returned first using Levenshtein edit distance.`},{question:`Why Levenshtein and not something simpler?`,answer:`Because the failure mode of simpler matching is real: prefix and exact search both fail on typos, which are common in real queries. Levenshtein handles the cases that matter, and the contact list is small enough that the computational cost is irrelevant.`},{question:`Was it a solo project?`,answer:`No. It was a six-person team project. The repository is com.ProGeeks.ContactsManager, and all six contributors are listed in the README.`}],related:[`vtt`,`sharp-money-manager`],language:`Java`},"academic-typing-training":{slug:`academic-typing-training`,label:`Case study · 2024–2025 · Client work · Kuwait`,headline:`A typing platform that cannot be cheated.`,lede:`A typing academy was running on a generic training tool where students could edit the page in DevTools to raise their own scores, and the teacher distributed every paragraph to every student by hand. The platform I built moved the score to the server and the workflow to the teacher.`,quickFacts:[{label:`Role`,value:`Sole engineer`},{label:`Duration`,value:`2024–2025`},{label:`Client`,value:`ATT (Academic Typing Training) · Kuwait`},{label:`Stack`,value:`.NET · Blazor · SQL Server`},{label:`Status`,value:`Paused by client`}],problem:{statement:`The training tool graded on the client. Every score was editable.`,explanation:`The academy was using a generic typing tool that calculated scores in the browser. Anyone with DevTools could open the page, change the number, and submit. The teacher could not trust the results. On top of that, assignments were distributed by hand — every paragraph copy-pasted to every student, every submission reviewed manually. The tool was making the work harder, not easier.`},approach:{title:`What I built.`,bullets:[`A full typing platform — classes, teachers, students, assignments, announcements, exams.`,`Score computation moved from the browser to the server. The client submits the raw input; the server produces the score.`,`Paragraph distribution became a single action — one assignment, one class, one push.`,`Bilingual Arabic and English, with RTL and LTR layouts.`,`Designed around the teacher, not the student. She was the one drowning in work.`],flow:[`Teacher`,`Assignment`,`Student Typing`,`Server Grade`,`Report`]},results:{title:`What shipped.`,stats:[{value:`Server`,label:`Grading authority`},{value:`1`,label:`Action to distribute an assignment`},{value:`AR · EN`,label:`Bilingual interface`}],note:`The platform launched and ran before the academy paused operations. The domain is still owned by the client.`},decisions:[{id:`ADR-001`,category:`Security`,title:`Server-side grading over client-side score submission`,problem:`The previous system calculated the score in JavaScript and submitted the final number. Anyone could open DevTools and submit whatever they wanted. The teacher had no way to know whether a score was real.`,options:`Trust the client · lock the UI with deterrents · re-grade the raw input on the server.`,choice:`The client submits the raw input — the typed text, the timing, the target paragraph — and the server computes the score. The browser never sends a number.`,consequence:`Forging a score requires forging a full typing record that matches the target text, which is the same as typing the text. The teacher could now trust every score in the system.`,rejected:`Locking the UI — blocking right-click, disabling DevTools, minifying the JavaScript — is a deterrent, not a fix. It assumes the browser is trustworthy, which it is not. Re-grading the raw input is the only version where the client cannot lie.`}],lessons:[{title:`Trust boundaries are cheaper than deterrents`,body:`The first instinct was to make cheating harder — hide the code, block the shortcuts, obfuscate the payload. The actual fix was to move the point of trust. Once the server was the authority, every deterrent became irrelevant.`},{title:`The teacher was the real user`,body:`The obvious build was for students — more practice modes, better keyboard animations. But the person drowning in work was the teacher. Designing around her workflow turned out to be the whole value proposition, not the student experience.`}],faqs:[{question:`What was Academic Typing Training?`,answer:`A typing academy in Kuwait. They needed a platform to replace a generic training tool that allowed students to fake their scores and required manual assignment distribution by the teacher.`},{question:`How did you stop students from faking scores?`,answer:`The client-side score was replaced with server-side grading. The browser submits the raw input — the typed text, the timing, the paragraph being typed — and the server computes the score. The client never sends a number.`},{question:`Is the platform still live?`,answer:`No. The academy paused operations. The domain is still owned by the client.`}],related:[`garage7`,`getop`]},garage7:{slug:`garage7`,label:`Case study · 2025 · Client work · Backend`,headline:`A backend for finding car parts and mechanics.`,lede:`Garage7 was the API behind a car-maintenance startup — the layer that would connect car owners to parts and mechanics. The team needed a backend engineer; the front-end was handled separately. This is what I built.`,quickFacts:[{label:`Role`,value:`Backend engineer`},{label:`Duration`,value:`2025`},{label:`Team`,value:`Multi-person · backend solo`},{label:`Stack`,value:`ASP.NET Core · EF Core · .NET`},{label:`Status`,value:`Delivered · not in production`}],problem:{statement:`Car owners have no single place to find parts and mechanics.`,explanation:`Finding the right part for a car means knowing the make, model, year, and variant. Finding a mechanic means knowing the service and the area. Both happen today through word of mouth, scattered forums, and phone calls. Garage7 was conceived as the API layer under an app that would bring both into one flow — parts, mechanics, orders, services, maintenance centers, vehicle records. The team had no specific pain point they were solving; they had a strong idea and a good fit for the role.`},approach:{title:`What I built.`,bullets:[`ASP.NET Core API with EF Core persistence.`,`Six domain areas: parts, mechanics, orders, services, maintenance centers, vehicle records.`,`Clean boundaries between domains — orders reference parts and services by identity, not by copy.`,`Built for a team — the front-end was handled separately.`]},results:{title:`What shipped.`,stats:[{value:`6`,label:`Domain areas`},{value:`1`,label:`API delivered`},{value:`Not shipped`,label:`Production state`}],note:`The API was delivered. The project was not taken to production.`},decisions:[{id:`ADR-001`,category:`Domain modelling`,title:`Six bounded contexts over one unified model`,problem:`Cars, parts, mechanics, orders, services, and maintenance centers are all related, but they change for different reasons and serve different queries. A single unified model couples them.`,options:`One unified model · six bounded contexts · hybrid with shared aggregates.`,choice:`Six contexts. Each has its own model and its own tables. Orders reference parts and services by identity.`,consequence:`Changes in one context do not cascade. A new field on parts does not require regenerating orders. The trade-off is that cross-context queries are joins, not in-model navigations.`,rejected:`A unified model is simpler to start with and much harder to evolve. The project was speculative — no live users, no feedback loop. The right choice was the one that would not rot if it survived.`}],lessons:[{title:`No pain point means no priority`,body:`The team had a strong idea and no real user yet. Without someone to say "this is the thing I need," every feature was equal weight and every order was opinion. I learned to ask early: who is this for, and what do they actually do today?`},{title:`Boundaries are cheapest before the first user`,body:`The domain split cost almost nothing during the build. It would have cost weeks after the first features shipped and the schema was populated. Boundaries early are cheap; boundaries later are rewrites.`}],faqs:[{question:`What is Garage7?`,answer:`Garage7 was the backend API for a car-maintenance startup — the layer that would connect car owners to parts and mechanics. It was a team project. The API was delivered; it was not brought to production.`},{question:`Why was it never shipped?`,answer:`The team did not reach the production stage. The API was delivered.`}],related:[`academic-typing-training`,`getop`],language:`C#`},getop:{slug:`getop`,label:`Case study · 2024 · Client work · Not shipped`,headline:`Multi-factor auth, built properly.`,lede:`GeTop was a platform for a software agency's website: users, client intake, internal team management. It was marked unviable and stopped before launch. What shipped before that was a complete multi-factor authentication flow, and that is what this case study covers.`,quickFacts:[{label:`Role`,value:`Sole engineer`},{label:`Duration`,value:`2024`},{label:`Stack`,value:`.NET · ASP.NET Core`},{label:`Focus`,value:`Authentication`},{label:`Status`,value:`Marked unviable · not shipped`}],problem:{statement:`Authentication for a B2B platform needs more than a password.`,explanation:`The platform would hold client data — project inquiries, contact information, internal staff notes. A password alone would not be enough for a security-conscious agency. The MFA flow needed to be real: setup, verification, recovery, enforcement. Not a checkbox on the roadmap.`},approach:{title:`What I built.`,bullets:[`Full multi-factor authentication flow — enroll, verify, recover.`,`TOTP as the second factor, RFC 6238.`,`Recovery codes as the fallback when the device is lost.`,`Enforcement policy — every internal user required, not opt-in.`,`The rest of the platform was in progress when the project was marked unviable.`],flow:[`Enroll`,`Verify`,`Use`,`Recover`]},results:{title:`What shipped.`,stats:[{value:`1`,label:`Full MFA flow`},{value:`TOTP`,label:`Second factor`},{value:`Not shipped`,label:`Project state`}],note:`The auth work was complete before the project was marked unviable. The rest of the platform was not.`},decisions:[{id:`ADR-001`,category:`Authentication`,title:`TOTP plus recovery codes over SMS as the second factor`,problem:`The second factor had to work without an SMS gateway, without a vendor, and without assuming every user has a smartphone at hand every time.`,options:`SMS one-time codes · TOTP app · email codes · push notification.`,choice:`TOTP plus recovery codes. TOTP is a standard that works with any authenticator app. Recovery codes handle the lost-device case.`,consequence:`No vendor dependency. No SMS delivery costs. The user brings their own authenticator. Recovery codes mean the support cost for a lost phone is one page, not a support ticket.`,rejected:`SMS is the most familiar to users and the weakest technically — SIM swaps, carrier attacks, delivery costs. Email codes are barely a factor at all. Push notifications require a native app or a vendor, neither of which the project had.`}],lessons:[{title:`Auth is a feature that has to be finished`,body:`A half-built MFA flow is worse than no MFA. If recovery does not work, users lock themselves out. If enrollment is skippable, they skip it. The work has to be complete or it should not ship at all.`},{title:`Unviable projects still ship learnings`,body:`GeTop never reached production. That does not make the auth work disappear. The MFA implementation is now the pattern I use for every project that needs it — it was worth building even though the platform around it was not.`}],faqs:[{question:`What is GeTop?`,answer:`GeTop was a platform for a software agency's website: users, client intake, internal team management. It was marked unviable and stopped before launch. What shipped before that was a full MFA system.`},{question:`Why include an unshipped project as a case study?`,answer:`Because the auth work is real engineering, and the reasoning behind it is worth documenting. Projects that do not ship still teach. That is worth showing.`}],related:[`garage7`,`academic-typing-training`],language:`C#`}};export{e as t};